1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,133
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. Last time, we ended on an uncomfortable

2
00:00:03,133 --> 00:00:03,933
fact:

3
00:00:03,933 --> 00:00:07,133
an agent that's stuck can look identical to one that succeeded, right

4
00:00:07,133 --> 00:00:08,133
up until someone checks.

5
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:09,700
Today:

6
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,767
three real mechanisms that turn 'the agent says it worked' into something

7
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:16,100
you can actually stand behind — and exactly where they stop working.

8
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:18,463
Here's the uncomfortable starting point.

9
00:00:18,863 --> 00:00:21,330
When an agent explains its reasoning — 'I checked the numbers,

10
00:00:21,697 --> 00:00:25,263
cross-referenced the filing, and concluded X' — that explanation was generated by

11
00:00:25,263 --> 00:00:27,230
the same model that generated the conclusion.

12
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:29,330
It's not a transcript of what happened.

13
00:00:29,697 --> 00:00:30,497
It's a

14
00:00:30,263 --> 00:00:32,463
plausible-sounding narration, produced after the fact.

15
00:00:32,830 --> 00:00:36,297
A model that hallucinated a number can write a perfectly coherent paragraph

16
00:00:36,297 --> 00:00:37,963
about how it derived that number.

17
00:00:38,663 --> 00:00:42,763
Fluency and accuracy are different properties, and a language model optimizes hard

18
00:00:42,763 --> 00:00:43,663
for the first one.

19
00:00:44,197 --> 00:00:47,997
The real question isn't whether the explanation sounds reasonable — it's whether

20
00:00:47,997 --> 00:00:50,730
any part of it can be checked against something outside the model

21
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:51,530
itself.

22
00:00:51,563 --> 00:00:54,963
The log is evidence of output. It is not evidence of process.

23
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:58,407
The first move is to stop treating the reasoning as one continuous

24
00:00:58,407 --> 00:01:02,407
story, and start treating it as a pile of individual, checkable assertions.

25
00:01:03,007 --> 00:01:05,407
Run the text through a parser that pulls out four kinds of

26
00:01:05,407 --> 00:01:06,207
claims:

27
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:08,207
citations, wherever the text points to a source;

28
00:01:08,573 --> 00:01:10,607
quantitative claims — dollar figures, percentages,

29
00:01:10,973 --> 00:01:12,207
multiples, basis points;

30
00:01:12,573 --> 00:01:16,307
hedge language — words like 'approximately' or 'assumed', flagging the model's own

31
00:01:16,307 --> 00:01:20,673
uncertainty; and causal claims — sentences using 'because' or 'resulting in', where

32
00:01:20,673 --> 00:01:24,507
the model asserts one thing caused another. Take a real line: 'Revenue

33
00:01:24,507 --> 00:01:28,207
grew 34 percent year over year, driven by international expansion — source:

34
00:01:28,207 --> 00:01:31,673
the 10-K.' The parser tags it three ways at once: quantitative, causal,

35
00:01:31,873 --> 00:01:32,673
and citation.

36
00:01:33,073 --> 00:01:35,140
None of this requires understanding what the text means.

37
00:01:35,573 --> 00:01:39,140
It's pattern matching — regular expressions hunting for percent signs, source

38
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:41,007
brackets, and a fixed list of hedge words.

39
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:44,340
You're not asking a second AI to judge the reasoning.

40
00:01:44,807 --> 00:01:48,707
You're mechanically converting prose into a checklist of falsifiable pieces.

41
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:52,220
Extraction gives you a list of claims. It doesn't tell you if

42
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,053
any of them are true — that's the next step, and it

43
00:01:54,053 --> 00:01:55,953
actually leaves the model's head entirely.

44
00:01:56,587 --> 00:01:59,253
Take that citation — 'source: the 10-K' — and the system fetches

45
00:01:59,253 --> 00:02:00,053
it for real.

46
00:02:00,287 --> 00:02:03,353
If it's a government filing, it parses the structured data directly: real

47
00:02:03,353 --> 00:02:07,087
reported numbers, not text scraped off a page. Otherwise, it searches the

48
00:02:07,087 --> 00:02:08,053
raw text for numbers.

49
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:10,887
Then it checks: does a matching number show up in that source,

50
00:02:11,487 --> 00:02:13,620
within about one percent, to allow for rounding?

51
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:17,087
A citation lands in one of three states: the source is reachable

52
00:02:17,087 --> 00:02:20,553
and a number matches; the source is reachable and nothing matches —

53
00:02:20,553 --> 00:02:24,253
a real red flag; or the fetch itself fails — unattainable, no

54
00:02:24,253 --> 00:02:25,653
judgment possible either way.

55
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:28,627
Roll all of that up into one number — the fraction of

56
00:02:28,627 --> 00:02:32,227
citations actually confirmed — and a human reviewer has something to act

57
00:02:32,227 --> 00:02:35,060
on that has nothing to do with how convincing the writing sounds.

58
00:02:35,727 --> 00:02:39,127
Say our 34 percent claim checks out against the real filing; across

59
00:02:39,127 --> 00:02:42,093
a whole reasoning trace, that might land at six of nine citations

60
00:02:42,093 --> 00:02:42,893
confirmed

61
00:02:42,927 --> 00:02:46,793
— sixty-seven percent. One honest caveat: the check isn't perfectly tight.

62
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:50,460
It pools every number in the whole reasoning trace and asks whether

63
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:53,027
any of them shows up in the source — so a citation

64
00:02:53,027 --> 00:02:56,727
can get marked confirmed on the strength of a completely unrelated number.

65
00:02:57,393 --> 00:03:00,427
That's a real gap, not a rounding error, and it's worth knowing

66
00:03:00,427 --> 00:03:02,193
before you trust the number too much.

67
00:03:03,233 --> 00:03:05,467
Verification catches claims that point at real sources.

68
00:03:05,933 --> 00:03:08,867
But plenty of reasoning doesn't cite anything external at all — it's

69
00:03:08,867 --> 00:03:12,533
synthesis, judgment, 'based on everything above, I conclude X.' You can't fetch

70
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:14,933
a source for that. So you use a different trick: ask the

71
00:03:14,933 --> 00:03:15,933
same question twice.

72
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:19,567
Run the identical query through the full reasoning process a second time,

73
00:03:19,767 --> 00:03:20,767
completely independently.

74
00:03:21,267 --> 00:03:23,967
Then compare the two conclusions — how much do the actual words

75
00:03:23,967 --> 00:03:28,200
overlap, and, weighted more heavily, how much do the specific numbers overlap.

76
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,933
A model reasoning from real evidence should land in roughly the same

77
00:03:31,933 --> 00:03:32,733
place twice.

78
00:03:33,533 --> 00:03:37,567
A model confabulating a plausible story tends to drift, especially on the

79
00:03:37,567 --> 00:03:41,733
numbers, because a fabricated number is much harder to reproduce identically than

80
00:03:41,733 --> 00:03:42,833
a fabricated vibe.

81
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:47,120
Score it, classify it: high agreement, medium, or low. And raise one

82
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,987
specific hard flag whenever a number shows up in one run but

83
00:03:49,987 --> 00:03:50,787
not the other at all.

84
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:54,753
Run our example twice: if 34 percent shows up both times, that's

85
00:03:54,753 --> 00:03:56,887
real signal — a high-agreement score, something like

86
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:01,153
0.82. If the second run says 41 percent instead, with no 34

87
00:04:01,353 --> 00:04:04,553
percent anywhere, that's a flag, not just a lower score — a

88
00:04:04,553 --> 00:04:06,387
number that only exists in one of two

89
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:10,520
otherwise-similar runs is one of the strongest signals available that it was

90
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:11,320
invented,

91
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:12,320
not retrieved.

92
00:04:12,990 --> 00:04:15,623
Here's the part that's tempting to skip, and the part that actually

93
00:04:15,623 --> 00:04:19,623
matters most. None of these three mechanisms proves the agent reasoned correctly.

94
00:04:20,123 --> 00:04:23,623
High consistency is weak positive evidence, not a guarantee — two runs

95
00:04:23,623 --> 00:04:26,957
can agree with each other while both being confidently, identically wrong.

96
00:04:27,523 --> 00:04:30,723
A model doesn't need real evidence to be consistent; it just needs

97
00:04:30,723 --> 00:04:33,157
to draw on the same internalized pattern twice.

98
00:04:33,957 --> 00:04:37,757
Verification only checks that a number exists somewhere in the cited source

99
00:04:37,923 --> 00:04:41,123
— it can't confirm the agent's causal story about what that number

100
00:04:41,123 --> 00:04:45,590
means. And a citation coming back unattainable tells you nothing either way.

101
00:04:46,270 --> 00:04:49,437
What these mechanisms are actually good at is the other direction: catching

102
00:04:49,437 --> 00:04:51,737
disagreement, catching absence, catching drift.

103
00:04:52,403 --> 00:04:55,903
Low consistency is strong negative evidence — it's very hard to accidentally

104
00:04:56,203 --> 00:04:57,537
look inconsistent while being right.

105
00:04:57,903 --> 00:05:00,503
A citation where the source is real but the claimed number simply

106
00:05:00,503 --> 00:05:03,137
isn't there is a real, structural problem, full stop.

107
00:05:03,637 --> 00:05:06,037
These tools reliably catch when something is wrong.

108
00:05:06,437 --> 00:05:09,703
They were never built to certify that something is right — treating

109
00:05:09,703 --> 00:05:12,837
them as if they could just relocates the trust problem one layer

110
00:05:12,837 --> 00:05:14,603
down instead of solving it.

111
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:16,827
So here's what actually holds up.

112
00:05:17,193 --> 00:05:20,027
One: don't grade the story, grade the claims — break reasoning into

113
00:05:20,027 --> 00:05:22,593
small, checkable pieces before you evaluate any of it.

114
00:05:23,027 --> 00:05:25,593
Two: check what can be checked against the outside world, and log

115
00:05:25,593 --> 00:05:28,827
honestly when something can't be checked at all, instead of quietly assuming

116
00:05:28,827 --> 00:05:29,627
pass.

117
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:30,560
Three:

118
00:05:30,427 --> 00:05:33,793
when nothing external exists to check against, make the system disagree with

119
00:05:33,793 --> 00:05:34,660
itself on purpose

120
00:05:35,027 --> 00:05:38,427
— genuine drift under repetition is one of the few signals a

121
00:05:38,427 --> 00:05:40,293
fabrication can't easily fake.

122
00:05:40,893 --> 00:05:44,060
None of these three prove truth. All three narrow what's plausible.

123
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,760
So: don't grade the story, grade the claims.

124
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:49,593
Check what can be checked against the outside world, and log it

125
00:05:49,593 --> 00:05:51,093
honestly when something can't be.

126
00:05:51,693 --> 00:05:54,793
And when nothing external exists to check, make the system disagree with

127
00:05:54,793 --> 00:05:57,593
itself on purpose — because genuine drift is one of the few

128
00:05:57,593 --> 00:05:59,327
things a fabrication can't fake.

129
00:05:59,827 --> 00:06:02,593
None of this proves truth. All of it narrows what's plausible.

130
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:05,527
Your turn. Take a real reasoning trace — one of your own

131
00:06:05,527 --> 00:06:07,127
agent's outputs, or a sample one.

132
00:06:07,627 --> 00:06:11,227
First, tag it by hand: mark every citation, every number, every hedge

133
00:06:11,227 --> 00:06:12,560
word, every causal claim.

134
00:06:12,993 --> 00:06:15,660
Then pick one citation and actually go check it against the real

135
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:18,527
source — did the number show up? If there's no external source

136
00:06:18,527 --> 00:06:21,593
to check, run the same prompt twice and see whether the specific

137
00:06:21,593 --> 00:06:23,293
numbers survive the second pass.

138
00:06:23,870 --> 00:06:26,503
That gap — between checking and proving — is exactly where the

139
00:06:26,503 --> 00:06:27,503
next problem lives:

140
00:06:27,903 --> 00:06:30,570
what happens when you've got more than one agent, and they don't

141
00:06:30,570 --> 00:06:31,370
agree at all.

142
00:06:31,637 --> 00:06:32,437
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
